!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/policy.tn?sha256=3a789b50ee0b8a9366317993e4e1038f871451dd532b154e33f6b71c0c9ffea3"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn?sha256=8c3cbece074056221cc28a40a6c6a91b205a8dd274d411d91dd304a303bf0356"
@doc:"""
  TSON processor policy — what a processor admits and spends (2026 Revision 40
  draft).

  A processor's policy is a property of the deployment, never of a schema
  ([TSON-DATA] §8.2): a schema that declared its own strictness would choose
  its own check. This document declares the policy's vocabulary, so a
  processor states the policy it judged under in one shape, and a deployment
  that writes its policy down writes a data document of type
  `policy.processor`. Such a document is named at the call site and never
  resolved by identity, and no document may name, import or otherwise select
  the policy it is judged under.

  `policy.identifier_policy` and `policy.script_policy` are §8.2's two
  policies, and `policy.limits` is §9.1's and [TSON-SCHEMA] §11.5's. A script
  is named by its Unicode Script property value alias (UAX #24), `Latin`,
  `Cyrillic`, `Han`.

  It imports meta.tn alone, every field typed by the kernel's vocabulary or by
  an entry of its own. Its entries are members of the schema map `policy`
  nested in its root, each named by its path from the root, and a schema
  whose import closure holds this document reserves that map name.
  """
!schema {
  policy => !schema {
    @doc:"UTS #39 §5.2's restriction levels, strictest first."
    restriction_level => !enum [
      ASCII_ONLY SINGLE_SCRIPT HIGHLY_RESTRICTIVE MODERATELY_RESTRICTIVE MINIMALLY_RESTRICTIVE UNRESTRICTED
    ]

    @doc:"""
      [TSON-DATA] §8.2's token policy: the restriction level over each whole
      token, and any script combinations admitted over the level (UTS #39
      §5.2's own device for Latn+Jpan and its siblings), each entry in
      `permitting` one such combination.
      """
    script_policy => { level: policy.restriction_level  permitting: [[text]] }

    @doc:"""
      [TSON-DATA] §8.2's identifier policy: the restriction level, whether it
      applies to each segment of a name rather than to the whole of it,
      whether no two names in one scope may share a UTS #39 skeleton, and any
      script combinations admitted over the level, as `policy.script_policy`
      states them.
      """
    identifier_policy => {
      level: policy.restriction_level
      per_segment: boolean
      skeleton_distinctness: boolean
      permitting: [[text]]
    }

    @doc:"""
      One resource limit: a count, a depth, or a size in code points, octets
      or bytes, a positive `int64`, 1 to 2^63-1. A processor that holds a
      limit in a narrower type than this range caps a larger value at the
      widest it holds.
      """
    limit => !int64 ^ { min: 1 }

    @doc:"""
      [TSON-DATA] §9.1's resource limits and [TSON-SCHEMA] §11.5's: what a
      processor will spend reading one document, and resolving one schema, as
      against what it admits as a name. Every limit defaults to the spec's own
      value, set at the tight end so that a document fitting it fits every
      processor above, and a deployment writes only what it raises. A limit
      refusal names the limit and the threshold, and is not a verdict on the
      document.

      The §9.1 limits bound a document, a schema document among them. Every
      limit is a `policy.limit`.
      The §11.5 limits bound the work resolution adds over a schema: the
      schemas one header reaches through `!!import`, the
      declarations in one schema map (instantiation entries
      included), the hops from a use site to a terminal entry, the length of
      one type's supertype chain, and the longest chain of waits one schema's
      resolution runs, an entry waiting on the next being ready.
      """
    limits => {
      max_depth?:                  policy.limit ~ 64
      max_token_length?:           policy.limit ~ 1048576
      max_text_length?:            policy.limit ~ 1048576
      max_numeric_literal_length?: policy.limit ~ 4096
      max_binary_size?:            policy.limit ~ 16777216
      max_document_size?:          policy.limit ~ 16777216
      max_elements?:               policy.limit ~ 1048576
      max_entries?:                policy.limit ~ 1048576
      max_fields?:                 policy.limit ~ 65536
      max_annotations?:            policy.limit ~ 64
      max_values?:                 policy.limit ~ 16777216
      max_import_closure?:         policy.limit ~ 64
      max_schema_entries?:         policy.limit ~ 65536
      max_alias_chain?:            policy.limit ~ 64
      max_supertype_chain?:        policy.limit ~ 64
      max_wait_depth?:             policy.limit ~ 64
    }

    @doc:"""
      The policy a processor judges a document under: §8.2's identifier and
      token policies and §9.1's limits. `unicode_data_version` is the UCD
      release whose tables the §8.2 rules read, which a report states beside
      the policy (§8.2) and a deployment's own policy document leaves out, the
      tables being the processor's rather than the deployment's choice.
      """
    processor => {
      identifier_policy: policy.identifier_policy
      token_policy: policy.script_policy
      unicode_data_version?: text
      limits: policy.limits
    }
  }
}
